<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  What the app is allowed to ask the operating system for.

  These matter because the bundle is signed with the **hardened runtime**. Under
  it, macOS refuses microphone access outright unless the signature carries
  `device.audio-input` — the `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription` string in
  Info.plist explains *why* the app wants the microphone, but this is what makes
  asking legal at all. A build missing this does not show a denied dialog; it
  simply never records.

  `disable-library-validation` is not optional either. The app loads ONNX
  Runtime and sherpa's dylibs out of `Contents/Frameworks/`, and those are
  third-party binaries signed by nobody. The hardened runtime otherwise refuses
  to load any library not signed by the same team, which would stop the app on
  its first attempt to transcribe.

  There is no entitlement for Screen Recording. That permission has no
  entitlement and no Info.plist key — it is granted only in System Settings,
  which is why `speaker.rs` has to explain it in prose instead.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Loading the unsigned third-party dylibs we bundle ourselves. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
